  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong lower-layer fit: the envelope cleanly carries opaque condition references and forbids speculative parsing, while mandatory proof slots bind the bytes; its main gap is that it does not itself define how geofencing or conditional-release eligibility is verified or recorded.",
    "strengths": [
      "Unknown pCID payloads can be stored and forwarded as exact bytes with an explicit unsupported-pCID outcome.",
      "Mandatory sig_pcid/sig_payload slots give the envelope a place to bind opaque condition references to integrity or proof material.",
      "Envelope, payload, and wrapper responsibilities are separated clearly, reducing accidental lower-layer overreach."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "It does not define how a node that cannot parse the condition vocabulary decides whether release is allowed.",
      "Peer-local promise accounting for withheld versus released content is left to higher-layer protocols.",
      "Human audit still depends on preserving and understanding external payload and proof handlers."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "A deployment could mistake successful opaque carriage or signature verification for authorization to release content.",
      "If pCID or sig_pcid handlers disappear, records may remain intact but lose practical auditability.",
      "Integrity of opaque bytes does not by itself prove the semantic truth of a geofencing condition."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Where should conditional-release proofs live: payload, sig_payload, or wrapper envelopes?",
      "What minimum verifier capability must Bob have before release is allowed rather than only store/forward?",
      "How should unknown sig_pcid or unevaluable proofs be captured in peer-local accounting?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
